.SH NAME
ktm-prelude \- In every program without an import: String, Error, EXIT_* and the integer limits.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
In every program without an import: String, Error, EXIT_* and the integer limits.
.SH TYPES
.SS Error
.EX
struct Error
{
  i64 code;
  u8 *message;
  u8 *file;
  i32 line;
};
.EE
.PP
What a throw carries and \fBcatch (Error e)\fR binds: the code (usually an enum member), a message (never NULL, "" without one), and the file and line of the throw. The message is freed when the catch block is left: copy it (cstr.dup) to keep it.
.SS String
.EX
struct String
{
  u8 *ptr;
  i64 len;
  i64 cap;
};
.EE
.PP
A string you own: its bytes (UTF-8 by convention) at ptr, len of them, room for cap, and always a NUL after the last one, so s.cstr() is free. Made empty (nothing allocated) by str.new() or a declaration, grown by its methods (module str's), freed by s.free(): \fBString s = str.new(); defer s.free();\fR\&. A String converts to a u8[] view of its bytes wherever one is expected. Its fields are read-only outside module str.
